      "text": "Let's solve the system of congruences step by step.\n\nFirst, consider the congruence \\(5c \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\\).\n\nTo isolate \\(c\\), we need the multiplicative inverse of 5 modulo 7. We need an integer \\(x\\) such that \\(5x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\). We can find this by testing values:\n\\[\n5 \\cdot 1 = 5 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\\[\n5 \\cdot 2 = 10 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n\\[\n5 \\cdot 3 = 15 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nSo, the inverse of 5 modulo 7 is 3. Multiplying both sides of the congruence \\(5c \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\\) by 3, we get:\n\\[\nc \\equiv 2 \\cdot 3 \\pmod{7} \\implies c \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nTherefore, \\(c = 6\\).\n\nNext, consider the congruence \\(6b \\equiv 3 + b \\pmod{7}\\).\n\nRearrange the congruence:\n\\[\n6b - b \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7} \\implies 5b \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nWe already know the inverse of 5 modulo 7 is 3. Multiplying both sides by 3, we get:\n\\[\nb \\equiv 3 \\cdot 3 \\pmod{7} \\implies b \\equiv 9 \\pmod{7} \\implies b \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nTherefore, \\(b = 2\\).\n\nFinally, substitute \\(b = 2\\) and \\(c = 6\\) into the first congruence \\(abc \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\):\n\\[\na \\cdot 2 \\cdot 6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\implies 12a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\implies 5a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nWe already know the inverse of 5 modulo 7 is 3. Multiplying both sides by 3, we get:\n\\[\na \\equiv 3 \\cdot 1 \\pmod{7} \\implies a \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nTherefore, \\(a = 3\\).\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder when \\(a + b + c\\) is divided by 7:\n\\[\na + b + c = 3 + 2 + 6 = 11\n\\]\nThe remainder when 11 is divided by 7 is:\n\\[\n11 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\nThus, the remainder when \\(a + b + c\\) is divided by 7 is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
