      "text": "To solve the given system of congruences, we will address each congruence step by step.\n\nFirst, consider the congruence:\n\\[ 5c \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\]\nWe need to find the multiplicative inverse of 5 modulo 7. The inverse of 5 is a number \\( x \\) such that \\( 5x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\). By testing values, we find that \\( 5 \\cdot 3 = 15 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\), so the inverse of 5 is 3. Therefore, we can multiply both sides of the congruence by 3:\n\\[ c \\equiv 2 \\cdot 3 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ c \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSo, \\( c = 6 \\).\n\nNext, consider the congruence:\n\\[ 6b \\equiv 3 + b \\pmod{7} \\]\nRearrange the terms to isolate \\( b \\):\n\\[ 6b - b \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 5b \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7} \\]\nAgain, we need the inverse of 5 modulo 7. From the previous step, we know that the inverse of 5 modulo 7 is 3. Therefore, we multiply both sides by 3:\n\\[ b \\equiv 3 \\cdot 3 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ b \\equiv 9 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ b \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSo, \\( b = 2 \\).\n\nNow, consider the congruence:\n\\[ abc \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSubstitute \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) with their values:\n\\[ a \\cdot 2 \\cdot 6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 12a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSimplify \\( 12 \\pmod{7} \\) to get \\( 5 \\):\n\\[ 5a \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\]\nWe need the inverse of 5 modulo 7, which is 3. Therefore, we multiply both sides by 3:\n\\[ a \\equiv 3 \\cdot 1 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ a \\equiv 3 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSo, \\( a = 3 \\).\n\nNow we have \\( a = 3 \\), \\( b = 2 \\), and \\( c = 6 \\). We need to find the remainder when \\( a + b + c \\) is divided by 7:\n\\[ a + b + c = 3 + 2 + 6 = 11 \\]\n\\[ 11 \\div 7 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 4 \\]\nSo, the remainder when \\( a + b + c \\) is divided by 7 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{4} \\]",
